ced Subgraphs and Tree Decompositions III. Three-Path-Configurations and Logarithmic Treewidth. (https://uwspace.uwaterloo.ca/items/f74a69a9-1a37-4ae7-bb55-d041f9109387)\nciteturn1search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4.2 years ago; Crawled: last week; 2109.01310.pdf (400.68 KB) ... We prove a conjecture of Sintiari and Trotignon, that there exists an absolute constant c for which every (theta, triangle)-free graph G has treewidth at most c log(jV(G)j). ... Our main result is in fact a generalization of the above conjecture, that treewidth is at most logarithmic in jV(G)j for every graph G excluding the so-called three-path-configurations as well as a fixed complete graph.\n\n# Induced Subgraphs and Tree Decompositions III. Three-Path-Configurations and Logarithmic Treewidth.\n\n## Files\n\n2109.01310.pdf (400.68 KB)\n\n## Date\n\n2022-09-09\n\n## Authors\n\nAbrishami, Tara\n\nChudnovsky, Maria\n\nHajebi, Sepehr\n\nSpirkl, Sophie\n\n## Publisher\n\nAdvances in Combinatorics\n\n## Abstract\n\nA theta is a graph consisting of two non-adjacent vertices and three internally disjoint paths between them, each of length at least two. For a family H of graphs, we say a graph G is H-free if no induced subgraph of G is isomorphic to a member of H. We prove a conjecture of Sintiari and Trotignon, that there exists an absolute constant c for which every (theta, triangle)-free graph G has treewidth at most c log(jV(G)j). A construction by Sintiari and Trotignon shows that this bound is asymptotically best possible, and (theta, triangle)-free graphs comprise the first known hereditary class of graphs with arbitrarily large yet logarithmic treewidth. Our main result is in fact a generalization of the above conjecture, that treewidth is at most logarithmic in jV(G)j for every graph G excluding the so-called three-path-configurations as well as a fixed complete graph. It follows that several NP-hard problems such as STABLE SET, VERTEX COVER, DOMINATING SET and COLORING admit polynomial time algorithms in graphs excluding the three-path-configurations and a fixed complete graph.\n\n## URI\n\nhttps://doi.org/10.19086/aic.2022.6 http://hdl.handle.net/10012/18756\n\n## Collections\n\nWaterloo ResearchCombinatorics and Optimization\n\n## Creative Commons license\n\nExcept where otherwise noted, this item's license is described as Attribution 4.0 International--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMatchings with few colors in colored complete graphs and hypergraphs (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/341081188_Matchings_with_few_colors_in_colored_complete_graphs_and_hypergraphs)\nciteturn1search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6.8 years ago; Crawled: 3 months ago; implies that we can span the two corresponding clusters with a matching in the color of the edge (1 or 2). ... Gyárfás, On the Ramsey number of disjoint hyperedges, MTA SZTAKI Working Paper August, 1983. ... Gyárfás, Large matchings with few colors, in: Problem 5 for the First ‘Emléktábla’ workshop, 2011, http://www.renyi.huemlektab/index. ... Terpai, Large 2-coloured matchings in 3-coloured complete hypergraphs, Electron. ... Given a graph G G and an integer t ≥ 2 $t\\ge 2$ determine the minimum number N N such that in any t t‐coloured complete graph on N N vertices there is a copy of G G using only edges of some t − 1 $t-1$ colours.\nClaim 11. For every atom with exactly two B’s, say A 1 B 2 B 3,|A 1 B 2 B 3|<√ε n and if it is >0, then |B 1 B 2 B 3|<√ε n.\n\nThen we proceed similarly with the possible cases.\n\n•Case 1.a’: 0 <|A 1 A 2 A 3|<√ε n. Then |B 1 B 2 B 3|<√ε n.Claim 9 implies that for every atom with exactly two A’s, say\n\nB 1 A 2 A 3, we have |B 1 A 2 A 3|<√ε n.Claim 11 implies that X′′\n\n2∪X′′\n\n3 covers B 1, apart from at most 4√ε n vertices.\n\n•Case 1.b’:|A 1 A 2 A 3| = 0,|B 1 B 2 B 3| ≥ √ε n.Claim 11 implies that no atom has exactly two B’s. Claim 10 implies that\n\nthere is at most one atom with exactly two A’s, say B 1 A 2